<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Javier Pérez de Cuéllar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/2020">2020</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Javier Pérez de Cuéllar was a Peruvian diplomat who served as the fifth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-general-of-the-united-nations">Secretary-General of the United Nations</a> from 1982 to 1991. He helped negotiate the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran-iraq-war">Iran–Iraq War</a> in 1988, the Soviet withdrawal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and the peace accords in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. He later ran unsuccessfully for the presidency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberto-fujimori">Alberto Fujimori</a> in 1995 and served as prime minister in 2000–2001.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/javier-perez-de-cuellar.jpg" alt="Javier Pérez de Cuéllar" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob Bogaerts for Anefo · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Javier_P%C3%A9rez_de_Cu%C3%A9llar_(1982).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Javier Pérez de Cuéllar was born on 19 January 1920 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, to a rentier family of Spanish descent whose ancestors had migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the sixteenth century. His father died when he was four. He attended the Colegio San Agustín, learned French from a governess and took a law degree at the Pontifical Catholic University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 1943. He joined the Ministry of Foreign Affairs in 1940 as an intern and the diplomatic service in 1944, serving first at Peru's embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where he married Yvette Roberts-Darricau in 1947.</p><p>He was a member of the Peruvian delegation to the first session of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1946, and later served as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and Venezuela.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1971 he became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s permanent representative to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. He sat on the Security Council in 1973 and 1974, presiding when the Cypriot coup took place in July 1974, and from 1975 to 1977 he was the Secretary-General's Special Representative in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. In 1979 he became under-secretary-general for Special Political Affairs and in 1981 the Secretary-General's personal representative on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. In December 1981 he was selected to succeed Kurt Waldheim as Secretary-General, and he was re-elected unanimously in October 1986.</p><p>As Secretary-General he mediated between the United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falklands-war">Falklands War</a>, supported the Contadora group in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> and in 1983 initiated the World Commission on Environment and Development. He worked on Namibian independence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran-iraq-war">Iran–Iraq War</a> and the Soviet withdrawal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, and in March 1986 formally accused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> of using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. His second term ended on 31 December 1991, after Boutros Boutros-Ghali was agreed as successor in November.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pérez de Cuéllar ran unsuccessfully against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberto-fujimori">Alberto Fujimori</a> for the presidency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 1995 and was prime minister and foreign minister from November 2000 to July 2001. He then served as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> until 2004. In 1997 he published a memoir of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> years, Pilgrimage for Peace. He received the Prince of Asturias Award for International Cooperation in 1987 and the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 1991. He died at his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 4 March 2020, two months after his hundredth birthday, the first secretary-general of the United Nations to become a centenarian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Javier Pérez de Cuéllar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Javier Pérez de Cuéllar</b> served as Secretary-General of the United Nations from 1982 to 1991.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-12.06,-77.04&amp;year=1985" aria-label="Lima on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2433 2613 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Javier Pérez de Cuéllar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2883 2883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2883 2883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-12.06,-77.04&amp;year=1985">What was here in 1985? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
